As promised, we will also enroll Microsoft's keys. For this purpose, we will
download their certificates, concatenate the db and KEK certificates together
respectively and create update files that can be enrolled together with our own
keys. The following certificates will be downloaded:
    - db certificates for verifying Microsoft Windows binaries
    - db certificates for verifying third-party binaries, such as UEFI drivers
        or option ROMs
    - KEK certificates so Windows may update the dbx and db variables for newer
        signed Windows images

Each of these include two certificates, one issued in 2011 (planned to be phased
out in 2026) and one issued in 2023. If you do not dual-boot, you technically
only need the db certificates for third-party binaries so that signed firmware
and drivers can be verified. But while it is unlikely, failing a signature check
could technically brick the device so it is best to err on the side of caution.

Furthermore, there is generally no downside to enrolling all of their keys.
Should you plan to use your personal keys for a criminal undertaking so
grand-scale that it comes to Microsoft's attention, they may revoke your keys.
In that case, upon booting Windows, they will use their KEKs to add your keys to
the dbx variable and your system will no longer be able to securely boot Linux.
If you have no such plans, there is no downside at all to enrolling all of their
keys.
